1.create docker-compose.yml file
  this is a docker config file, it is a handwritten recipe
  we set all images that our application need here.

  we will write any images we want under the "SERVICES"
  each image has some properties like port, container_name, volume,...
  (a volume is Docker-managed disk storage that survives container restarts. This is where your tables live.)

2."docker compose up -d"                                                 -d = detached (runs in background). First run pulls/uses the image; you'll see "Container easyfactor-postgres Started".
  this command will read our docker-compose.yaml
  and create everything it described.

Note: we have the image locally,
      but if we don't have it, this command will download it automatically,
      this images will placed in Docker's own storage (inside the Docker Desktop VM's disk on macOS).
      Your docker-compose.yml only names the image; the bytes live in
      Docker's library. docker images lists what's cached locally.

Note: we can download it manually by "docker pull postgres:17"

3.check the container work properly
  "docker compose ps"               NAME                  IMAGE         COMMAND                  SERVICE    CREATED         STATUS         PORTS
                                    easyfactor-postgres   postgres:17   "docker-entrypoint.s…"   postgres   9 minutes ago   Up 9 minutes   0.0.0.0:5432->5432/tcp

4.Check Postgres inside the container is ready
  "docker exec easyfactor-postgres pg_isready -U postgres"
                                            /var/run/postgresql:5432 - accepting connections

Note: this command will execute inside the container,
      so it will execute on postgres

Note: "pg_isready -U postgres" is postgres command,
      it will check PostGres is ready or not!
      -U postgres means this command will execute by postgres user